1790667571.126	The history of human civilization is intrinsically linked to the ability to store, replicate, and disseminate knowledge. Before the invention of mechanical reproduction, information traveled slowly, laboriously, and expensively. Books were luxury items, manuscripts painstakingly copied by scribes in monasteries or scriptoriums—acts that limited literacy to the elite few. The bottleneck was not intellectual curiosity; it was technological capability. This profound constraint on the sharing of ideas would fundamentally alter the trajectory of human thought, religion, science, and governance.
1790667571.126	
1790667571.126	At the heart of this transformation lies one invention: the printing press. Far from being a singular, sudden epiphany in 1450, the printing press is a cumulative achievement spanning millennia—a confluence of metallurgy, mathematics, woodworking, and chemistry across multiple global cultures. Its history is not merely a technical narrative but a rich tapestry woven through cultural exchange, technological iteration, economic necessity, and revolutionary social upheaval.
1790667571.126	
1790667571.126	### Part I: Precursors to Mechanical Reproduction (Antiquity to the Medieval Period)
1790667571.126	
